  "content": {
    "id": "p23-q13-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "13",
    "prompt": "The area of the largest equilateral triangle shown below is 1. Each orange triangle has a side length that is half the side length of the previous (larger) triangle.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q13-diagram",
        "role": "question",
        "format": "tikz",
        "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"geometry\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[{\"name\":\"orangeArea\",\"hex\":\"FCE8D5\",\"reason\":\"identifies the orange triangles whose areas are requested\"}],\"reference\":\"source page 23, p23-q13-diagram\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=2cm,y=2cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\definecolor{orangeArea}{HTML}{FCE8D5}\n\\path[use as bounding box] (0,-1) rectangle ({sqrt(3)},1);\n\\foreach \\k in {0,...,5} {\n\\pgfmathsetmacro{\\u}{sqrt(3)/pow(2,\\k+1)}\n\\pgfmathsetmacro{\\v}{1/pow(2,\\k+1)}\n\\path[fill=orangeArea] (\\u,\\v)--({2*\\u},0)--(\\u,-\\v)--cycle;\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (\\u,\\v)--({2*\\u},0)--(\\u,-\\v)--cycle;\n}\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0)--({sqrt(3)},1)--({sqrt(3)},-1)--cycle;\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
        "src": null,
        "widthMm": 35.8,
        "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
        "overlayOf": null,
        "transparent": false,
        "axes": null,
        "spec": {
          "sourcePage": 23,
          "sourceRegion": "Question 13, upper-left diagram",
          "description": "A left-pointing equilateral triangle contains a sequence of right-pointing orange equilateral triangles decreasing towards its left vertex. Light orange distinguishes the requested regions; ordinary outlines are black.",
          "mathematics": "The largest orange triangle joins the midpoints of the outer triangle. Each subsequent orange triangle has half the preceding side length and one quarter its area.",
          "answerVisibility": "Only the supplied construction is visible; no area values or solution annotations are shown."
        },
        "derived": false,
        "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
      }
    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q13-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the area of the largest orange triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac14$",
          "worked": "The largest orange triangle has half the side length of the outer triangle. Areas of similar triangles scale by the square of the side-length ratio.\n$$\\begin{align*}A_1&=1\\times\\left(\\frac12\\right)^2\\\\A_1&=\\frac14.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q13-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the area of the second largest orange triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac1{16}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}A_2&=\\frac14\\times\\left(\\frac12\\right)^2\\\\A_2&=\\frac1{16}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q13-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, find the total area of all the orange triangles.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac13$\n\nThe areas form a geometric series with first term $\\frac14$ and common ratio $\\frac14$.",
          "worked": "The areas form a geometric series with $a=\\frac14$ and $r=\\frac14$. Since $|r|<1$,\n$$\\begin{align*}S&=\\frac{a}{1-r}\\\\S&=\\frac{\\frac14}{1-\\frac14}\\\\S&=\\frac13.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q13-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Use the diagram to give an argument that\n$$\\frac14+\\frac1{4^2}+\\frac1{4^3}+\\cdots=\\frac13.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The outer triangle can be partitioned into three congruent stacks of triangles like the orange stack. Each stack therefore has one third of its area.",
          "worked": "Joining the outer triangle's side midpoints produces four congruent triangles. One is orange; apply the same subdivision to the leftward remainder repeatedly. The regions above and below the orange stack can each be subdivided into a congruent copy of that stack. These three stacks fill the outer triangle, whose area is 1. Thus the orange stack has area $\\frac13$.\n$$\\frac14+\\frac1{4^2}+\\frac1{4^3}+\\cdots=\\frac13.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
    ]
  },
